---
/**
 * /services/garage-cleanouts — the first service page on the 2026 template.
 *
 * ─────────────────── Why this is its own route file ───────────────────
 *
 * It exists to keep legacy.css off this page, and nothing else.
 *
 * The redesign is rolling out one service at a time, so for a while two template
 * systems have to coexist. When the shared [service] route imported both, Astro
 * put BOTH stylesheets in this page's bundle — the module graph is what decides
 * the CSS, not which template actually renders — and legacy's unscoped rules
 * loaded after base.css and reached into the new layout. `.hero h1` painted the
 * headline white on bone; `.cta` put a green slab behind the buttons. Defending
 * with specificity worked but left the new template permanently braced against a
 * stylesheet it should never have seen, and twelve legacy selectors still
 * matched somewhere in its tree.
 *
 * A static route file is the fix, because Astro bundles per page module. This
 * page imports only ServiceLayout2026, so only the new system's CSS ships with
 * it. The [service] route now excludes this slug from getStaticPaths, so there
 * is exactly one page for the URL and no route collision.
 *
 * ─────────────────────────── Rollout ───────────────────────────
 *
 * Each service that moves over gets a sibling file like this one and is added to
 * REDESIGNED in the [service] route. When the last one migrates, delete all of
 * them, point the [service] route at ServiceLayout2026, and drop ServiceLayout
 * and legacy.css with it — at which point the isolation is free rather than
 * arranged.
 */
import ServiceLayout2026 from '../../../layouts/ServiceLayout2026.astro';
import { services } from '../../../data/services';

const service = services.find((s) => s.slug === 'garage-cleanouts');

if (!service) {
  throw new Error(
    'services.ts no longer contains "garage-cleanouts", which this route renders.',
  );
}
---

<ServiceLayout2026 {service} />
